, "effective_scope": "historical/background only", "kind": "data-quality alert", "record": "Support-12-11", "review_note": "Record 11 must be checked for scope and authority. It may resemble the live case, but only explicitly current material outside this archive governs. Similar names, matching keywords, and a retained status do not make historical facts operative; verify the effective date and exact case identifier.", "status": "reference"}, {"detail": "A digest groups five support cases by keyword. It warns that keyword matches are not policy mappings and that each case still requires exact alias and effective-version lookup.", "effective_scope": "historical/background only", "kind": "related-case digest", "record": "Support-12-12", "review_note": "Record 12 must be checked for scope and authority. It may resemble the live case, but only explicitly current material outside this archive governs. Similar names, matching keywords, and a retained status do not make historical facts operative; verify the effective date and exact case identifier.", "status": "reference"}, {"detail": "An external bulletin recommends a more permissive treatment of Willow Legacy. The organization did not adopt it, and the bulletin is stored only as background material.", "effective_scope": "historical/background only", "kind": "vendor bulletin", "record": "Support-12-13", "review_note": "Record 13 must be checked for scope and authority. It may resemble the live case, but only explicitly current material outside this archive governs. Similar names, matching keywords, and a retained status do not make historical facts operative; verify the effective date and exact case identifier.", "status": "archived"}, {"detail": "Two reviewers acknowledged the current source hierarchy. Their acknowledgement validates publication controls but is not an approval of this individual request.", "effective_scope": "historical/background only", "kind": "approval trail", "record": "Support-12-14", "review_note": "Record 14 must be checked for scope and authority. It may resemble the live case, but only explicitly current material outside this archive governs. Similar names, matching keywords, and a retained status do not make historical facts operative; verify the effective date and exact case identifier.", "status": "reference"}, {"detail": "This archive segment is retained through 2030 for reproducibility. Retention status means the record remains readable, not that an obsolete value remains operative.", "effective_scope": "historical/background only", "kind": "retention marker", "record": "Support-12-15", "review_note": "Record 15 must be checked for scope and authority. It may resemble the live case, but only explicitly current material outside this archive governs. Similar names, matching keywords, and a retained status do not make historical facts operative; verify the effective date and exact case identifier.", "status": "reference"}], "channel_rules": {"email": "requires token check", "portal": "authenticated"}, "coverage": {"W1": "30-day support; recurrence extension x"}, "footnotes": {"x": "Grandfathered W1 receives 60 days for class R only if a remote patch for the same class was completed during the first 30 days and contact is authenticated."}, "issues": {"echo": "audio recurrence class R"}, "patch_log": {"day 20": "class R remote patch completed"}, "plans": {"Willow Legacy": "plan W1, cohort grandfathered"}, "service_case": {"activation": "day 0", "authentication": "no token check recorded", "contact_channel": "email", "issue_alias": "echo", "plan_name": "Willow Legacy", "prior_fix": "remote patch completed day 20", "reported": "day 41"}, "summary": "A call-center note saying support expired at day 30 did not inspect cohort or recurrence."}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-02", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-02", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 267, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The request is timely, but the product-box factory seal is split. Under the supplied definition, the product is not unopened regardless of the customer's everyday-language statement.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "The customer's quoted claim and timely return invite acceptance, but the defined term turns on seals. A second plausible evidence source must be classified under the governing source hierarchy."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The stated condition is not satisfied under the operative facts.", "true": "The stated condition is satisfied under the operative facts."}, "instructions": "Is this return eligible for the instant unopened-item refund?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "Aster Lane’s refund desk has a rule: a shipment is “unopened” only if every factory seal remains intact. A torn outer courier bag is irrelevant. Customer Len writes, “I never opened the parcel, although the clear seal over the product box split when I lifted it from the wet mailer.” An intake photo shows the courier bag torn, the cardboard product box closed, and its clear factory seal split end to end. The agent’s note says, “Customer says unopened; likely eligible.” Eligibility for the instant unopened-item refund requires return within 30 days and an unopened product. The return was requested on day 12. A separate defect route may apply after inspection, but the question concerns the instant unopened-item route only.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Inspection protocol P-8 defines “factory sealed” by every manufacturer-applied seal being continuous at inspection. A reseller wrap and an intact inner moisture strip are evidence of condition but cannot replace a split factory seal. The intake badge saying SEALED is generated from the seller declaration, not inspection."}
{"expected": "no", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-04", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-04", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 161, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Jo identified the pro-shipping words as Sam's quoted statement and expressly gave the opposite personal recommendation. Confirming Sam's view does not adopt it.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "A literal transcript contains Jo speaking an enthusiastic command, but its quotation scope matters."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The stated condition is not satisfied under the operative facts.", "true": "The stated condition is satisfied under the operative facts."}, "instructions": "Did Jo endorse shipping the proposal immediately?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "QuillWorks evaluates whether a reviewer endorsed a proposal. In the meeting transcript, reviewer Jo says: “Sam wrote in the draft chat, ‘This plan is flawless; ship it now.’ I am quoting that because it captures exactly the overconfidence we must avoid. My own recommendation is not to ship until the load test passes.” Later Jo answers “correct” when asked whether Sam had favored immediate shipment. The minutes bot extracts the sentence “This plan is flawless; ship it now” and attributes it to Jo because Jo spoke the words aloud. The rubric asks about Jo’s own recommendation, not whether Jo accurately reported another person’s position."}
{"expected": 0, "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-05", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-05", "labels": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 313, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The authorization was withdrawn before any reliance. Section 7 makes the later copied note evidentiary only, standard validation was used, and the parcel was not released, so level 0 applies.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "2", "why_hard": "The decisive withdrawal and non-revival rule must be found in a broader hierarchy and applied against a later warehouse note."}, "question": {"criteria": ["No operative exception.", "Requested but unauthorized exception.", "Authorized, unused exception.", "Authorized exception already used."], "instructions": "Assign the current fulfillment-exception level.", "type": "score"}, "split": "public", "state": "Morrow Books — fulfillment-control extract, revision 6. Section 2 assigns exception levels: 0 means no operative exception; 1 means requested but unauthorized; 2 means authorized but unused; 3 means used for a shipment. Section 4 says an authorization may be withdrawn until the warehouse first relies on it by bypassing validation or releasing the parcel. Section 7 says packing notes are evidentiary only: copying an authorization does not itself constitute reliance, and a note copied after withdrawal does not restore authority. A portal address correction processed normally is not use of an exception.\n\nCase 44: On 3 May a supervisor authorized one address override. At 08:15 on 4 May the customer corrected the address through the ordinary portal. At 08:22 the supervisor wrote, “Withdraw the override; standard address validation now applies.” At 08:31 a clerk, working from yesterday's queue, copied the authorization into the packing note and marked “override ready.” The validation log at 08:44 shows the corrected address passed standard validation; bypass_used=false. The parcel remains in the cage. A shift summary calls the order “authorized for override,” but it was generated from packing-note text rather than the authority register."}
{"expected": "keep_open_unresolved", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-06", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-06", "labels": ["close_confirmed", "keep_open_unresolved", "close_agent_verified", "await_internal_metrics"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 267, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The customer never reported that the dashboard worked and later reported the opposite. Under sections 5–8, backend telemetry and synthetic success cannot replace confirmation about an interface-visible defect, so the ticket stays open.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "close_confirmed", "why_hard": "Conditional consent, healthy telemetry, an automated resolution card, and a synthetic success all compete with the controlling customer-interface report."}, "question": {"criteria": {"await_internal_metrics": "Neither close nor keep open until another telemetry window.", "close_agent_verified": "Close because agent-side telemetry alone meets the stated closure rule.", "close_confirmed": "Close because the customer confirmed actual resolution.", "keep_open_unresolved": "Keep open because customer reports the issue persists."}, "instructions": "Choose the correct ticket disposition.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "Bright Orchard support handbook, sections 5–8. A ticket may close when the customer reports the affected function working after remediation. An advance statement such as “if X fixes it, close” records conditional consent, not a report that X worked. Agent telemetry can corroborate a customer report but cannot replace one for an interface-visible defect. If reports conflict, evidence about the customer's affected interface outranks backend health; automated summaries never establish customer confirmation.\n\nTicket BO-771 concerns a dashboard that renders blank for the customer. At 10:02 the customer wrote, “If resetting the gateway fixes it, you can close this.” The agent reset it at 10:14. Gateway telemetry was normal from 10:16 through 11:16, and an automated card displayed RESOLUTION LIKELY. At 11:18 the agent wrote “Reset worked, closing as authorized.” At 11:23 the customer replied from the affected account, “The dashboard is still blank for me. Please do not close.” A synthetic test at 11:25 loaded the dashboard, but no later customer report exists."}
{"expected": "late", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-08", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-08", "labels": ["on_time", "late", "indeterminate", "canceled"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 188, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The unflagged gate scan controls and records 18:04, which is after the inclusive 18:00 cutoff. Forecasts and lower-priority accounts do not override it.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "on_time", "why_hard": "The frozen ON TIME banner, prediction, and handwritten 17:59 all point toward the tempting status. A second plausible evidence source must be classified under the governing source hierarchy."}, "question": {"criteria": {"canceled": "The trip did not arrive.", "indeterminate": "No authoritative actual time can be selected.", "late": "Authoritative actual arrival is after 18:00.", "on_time": "Authoritative actual arrival is at or before 18:00."}, "instructions": "What final punctuality status applies?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "Vela Transit awards the “on-time” status when arrival is no later than 18:00 at the destination clock. Dispatch predicted 17:52. A monitoring banner at 17:55 read “ON TIME,” but banners are forecasts and freeze once a trip enters the tunnel. The actual gate scan, which is the authoritative arrival record, occurred at 18:04. A passenger message says the bus reached the station “around six,” and the driver’s handwritten log says 17:59; policy resolves discrepancies in favor of the gate scan unless it is flagged faulty. No fault flag exists.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Timing standard T4 uses the first unflagged physical gate scan. Manual estimates, scans marked TEST, and dispatch-board projections do not substitute; equality with the deadline is on time."}
{"expected": "blocked_second_review", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-10", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-10", "labels": ["ready_one_review", "ready_two_reviews", "blocked_second_review", "blocked_failed_test"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 129, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Changing the timeout makes P-7 behavioral, so two approvals are required. Reviewer B's conditional statement did not become an approval automatically when the test passed.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "ready_two_reviews", "why_hard": "Both the documentation label and fulfilled condition encourage counting a review that was never actually given."}, "question": {"criteria": {"blocked_failed_test": "Blocked because the required timeout test failed.", "blocked_second_review": "Blocked because a behavioral change lacks a second approval.", "ready_one_review": "Ready because only one review is required.", "ready_two_reviews": "Ready because two valid approvals exist."}, "instructions": "Choose P-7's release readiness.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "Granite Finch requires two independent reviewers unless the change is documentation-only. The release note calls patch P-7 “documentation cleanup,” but the diff summary says it changes a timeout constant from 30 to 45 seconds and fixes three comments. Classification follows changed behavior, not the release-note title. Reviewer A approved. Reviewer B wrote, “I would approve after the timeout test passes.” The test passed later, but Reviewer B never sent a new approval; conditional intentions are not approvals."}
{"expected": "no", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-13", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-13", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 175, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The deletion language is inside the editor's quoted question, and the author expressly rejects it and requests retention.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "Keyword extraction turns an attributed question into an apparent command. A second plausible evidence source must be classified under the governing source hierarchy."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The condition in the question is not established by the operative facts.", "true": "The condition in the question is established by the operative facts."}, "instructions": "Did the author request deletion of chapter 6?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "Lumen Press asks whether an author requested deletion of chapter 6. The author's message says, “My editor asked, ‘Should we delete chapter 6?’ Please do not take that quoted question as my instruction. I want chapter 6 retained, but delete the duplicate appendix.” An extraction system lists “delete chapter 6” as a detected imperative after dropping punctuation and attribution. The decision must follow the author's communicative intent in context.\n\nPolicy excerpt: Editorial deletion requires an affirmative instruction identifying the material to remove. Discussion of a possible cut, quoted advice, and an instruction to retain the chapter are interpreted by the latest direct author instruction."}
{"expected": "no", "family": "trap", "group": "hard-sol-a-trap-15", "id": "hard-sol-a-trap-15", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 256, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The 07:30 notice was withdrawn before 09:00. Reinstatement counts as a new notice, and the 09:20 message is too late; no emergency-review basis is supplied, so the absence is not excused.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "Receipt acknowledgement, display order, withdrawal, reinstatement timing, and a separate unused emergency route must be distinguished."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The condition in the question is not established by the operative facts.", "true": "The condition in the question is established by the operative facts."}, "instructions": "Is the absence excused under the guardian-notice rule?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "Nacre School attendance policy, revision 2026-2. A guardian notice excuses an absence if it is operative before the scheduled class start. A guardian may withdraw a notice before that start; a withdrawn notice is then treated as absent. Reinstatement is a new notice and uses its own receipt time. Messages sent after the scheduled start cannot satisfy the advance-notice route, although the separate emergency-review route may be used when an emergency is documented. Office acknowledgements record receipt only and do not prevent withdrawal.\n\nFor Toma's 09:00 class, a guardian submitted “Toma will be absent; please excuse” at 07:30. The office acknowledged it at 07:42. At 08:10 the same authenticated guardian wrote, “Please disregard my excuse; Toma will attend.” Toma did not attend. At 09:20 the guardian wrote, “I should not have withdrawn it; please use the first message.” No emergency or documentation is claimed. The attendance screen still shows the 07:30 message first because it sorts by conversation start."}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-01", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-01", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 137, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The two-equation elimination is valid: 3,508−2,640=868, so 124 adults and 116 students. Both requested checks are explicitly correct.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": null, "why_hard": "The integer solution uses numbers that invite arithmetic slips, but the response is fully correct."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "A theater sold 240 tickets. Adult tickets cost $18 and student tickets cost $11. Total receipts were $3,508. Determine the number of each ticket type, and show an equation check using both the count and revenue totals. Do not merely state the result.", "response": "Let a be adult tickets and s be student tickets. Then a+s=240 and 18a+11s=3508. Subtracting 11(a+s)=2640 from the revenue equation gives 7a=868, so a=124 and s=116. Checks: 124+116=240; 18(124)+11(116)=2232+1276=3508. Thus 124 adult and 116 student tickets were sold."}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-02", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-02", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 96, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The loss is 13.5 L, leaving 71.5 L, and the later addition produces 83.5 L, not 84.5 L. The final addition is wrong despite a correct chronological setup.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "A fluent chain contains a one-unit addition error at the last step."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "A tank starts with 85 liters. It leaks 1.8 liters per hour for 7.5 hours, then 12 liters are added. Report the final volume in liters to one decimal place and show the calculation in chronological order.", "response": "The leak removes 1.8 × 7.5 = 13.5 liters. After the leak the tank has 85 − 13.5 = 71.5 liters. Adding 12 liters gives 84.5 liters. Final volume: 84.5 L."}}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-05", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-05", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 150, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "It validates n, accepts and consumes a one-shot iterable exactly once, returns consecutive lists of at most n items, handles a final partial chunk, and returns an empty list for empty input.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": null, "why_hard": "The eager list result is required and the implementation must still be checked against one-shot iteration, validation, emptiness, and final partial chunks."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "Implement Python function chunks(xs, n) returning consecutive lists of at most n items. Requirements: raise ValueError when n <= 0; accept any iterable, including a one-shot generator; an empty iterable returns an empty list.", "response": "```python\ndef chunks(xs, n):\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError('n must be positive')\n    result = []\n    current = []\n    for item in xs:\n        current.append(item)\n        if len(current) == n:\n            result.append(current)\n            current = []\n    if current:\n        result.append(current)\n    return result\n```"}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-08", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-08", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 89, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The squared factor and unrounded product are correct, but 23.22576 rounds to 23.226 at three decimal places, not 23.225.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "A correct dimensional method hides a last-digit rounding error."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "Convert 250 square feet to square meters using exactly 1 foot = 0.3048 meter. Show how the squared conversion factor is applied and give the answer to three decimal places.", "response": "Because the measurement is area, use the factor twice: 250 ft² × (0.3048 m/ft)² = 250 × 0.09290304 m² = 23.22576 m². To three decimal places: 23.225 m²."}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-10", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-10", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 112, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The first seven listed dates are right, but Monday December 8 is the eighth business day. The response skips that ordinary Monday and incorrectly lands on Tuesday December 9.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "Two adjacent holidays across a weekend make an additional unjustified skipped weekday easy to overlook."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "Starting Monday, November 24, 2025, add 8 business days. Count the start as day 0; business days are Monday-Friday, and Thursday November 27 and Friday November 28 are holidays. State the resulting date and list the counted business days.", "response": "Counted days are Tue Nov 25 (1), Wed Nov 26 (2), Mon Dec 1 (3), Tue Dec 2 (4), Wed Dec 3 (5), Thu Dec 4 (6), Fri Dec 5 (7), and Tue Dec 9 (8). Result: Tuesday, December 9, 2025."}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-14", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-14", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 179, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The response retains four requested facts but replaces the required inconclusive finding with a claim that the improvement is proven. That directly contradicts the source's failure to reach the significance threshold.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "Accurate metrics and a confident recommendation can distract from the omitted and reversed uncertainty fact."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "Write a two-sentence executive summary retaining all of these facts: pilot involved 38 stores; conversion rose from 4.1% to 4.8%; returns were unchanged; the test ran six weeks; results are not yet statistically conclusive. Source: A six-week checkout pilot across 38 stores produced a measured conversion change from 4.1 percent to 4.8 percent. Return rates held steady. Because traffic was lower than forecast, the analysis has not reached the pre-registered significance threshold.", "response": "A six-week pilot in 38 stores raised conversion by 0.7 percentage points, from 4.1% to 4.8%, while return rates stayed unchanged. The improvement proves the new checkout should be rolled out chain-wide."}}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-15", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-15", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 111, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "All arithmetic follows the stated order: 72 total, 60 available, and divmod by five is 12 remainder zero.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": null, "why_hard": "The zero remainder may look suspicious in a problem framed around leftovers, but it is correct."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "A bakery makes 3 batches of 24 rolls. Twelve rolls are reserved for an order. The rest are packed equally into 5 display baskets, with any leftovers kept in the kitchen. State rolls per basket and leftovers, showing integer division.", "response": "There are 3 × 24 = 72 rolls total. After reserving 12, 60 remain. Integer division gives 60 ÷ 5 = 12 remainder 0, so each basket receives 12 rolls and none are left in the kitchen."}}
{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-18", "id": "hard-sol-b-judge_hard-18", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 88, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The record fields are correct, but the response contains a newline after 0027. The request expressly requires the output to end immediately after 0027 with no trailing whitespace, so the response is inadequate.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "The visible record is correct, but an explicit byte-boundary requirement makes the normally ignored trailing newline substantive."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response has any substantive error or misses any explicit requirement.", "true": "The response fully and correctly satisfies every explicit request, including method, edge cases, units, formatting, and retained facts."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy the request?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "Return a semicolon-separated record with fields name, active, quota in that order. Exact output rules: no spaces; active is lowercase `true`; quota is the zero-padded four-digit integer 27; name is `north`. Output only the record, ending immediately after `0027` with no trailing newline or whitespace.", "response": "north;true;0027\n"}}
{"expected": "reject_entire_claim", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-01", "id": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-01", "labels": ["approve_all", "approve_meals_only", "approve_lodging_only", "reject_entire_claim"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3431, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The claim arrived after 20 business days from the final travel day, even excluding weekends and both closures, and the portal-outage exception does not apply. Section 7 makes rejection mandatory and section 6 says pre-approval cannot waive timeliness. The later amendment would also cap lodging at 190, while the taxi pre-approval remains valid; those merits cannot rescue an untimely entire claim.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "approve_all", "why_hard": "The decisive outcome requires combining a defined term, a baseline rule, a narrow exception, and a later controlling amendment across a long document."}, "question": {"criteria": {"approve_all": "The case results in approve all under all controlling provisions.", "approve_lodging_only": "The case results in approve lodging only under all controlling provisions.", "approve_meals_only": "The case results in approve meals only under all controlling provisions.", "reject_entire_claim": "The case results in reject entire claim under all controlling provisions."}, "instructions": "Apply the controlling document and select the single authorized outcome for the case file.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "TRAVEL REIMBURSEMENT MANUAL — revision 7\n1. Purpose and order of authority. This manual governs employee travel reimbursements submitted from 1 April 2026. Defined terms in section 2 apply everywhere, including appendices. If a case-specific written pre-approval conflicts with the standard limits, the pre-approval controls only where section 6 permits it. Amendment A at the end was adopted later than all numbered sections and controls any inconsistency.\n\n2. Definitions. A \"travel day\" is a calendar day on which the employee is away from the home work location at midnight, except the departure day is also a travel day even when departure is after midnight local time due to a delayed carrier. A \"provided meal\" is food included without separately stated cost in lodging, conference registration, or carrier service; dietary unsuitability does not stop it being provided unless the accessibility office documented the restriction before travel. \"Remote-site travel\" means the destination is more than 80 road kilometers from both the employee's home and assigned office. \"Timely\" means received in the expense portal within 20 business days after the final travel day. Business days exclude weekends and the company closure dates listed in section 9.\n\n3. Meal allowance. The standard allowance is 68 credits per full travel day. Departure and return days receive 75 percent. Deduct 20 percent for a provided breakfast, 30 percent for lunch, and 50 percent for dinner; percentages are of the unadjusted 68-credit daily allowance. The daily result cannot be below zero. Receipts neither increase nor decrease this allowance.\n\n4. Lodging. Lodging is reimbursable up to 190 credits per night. Taxes stated separately are outside that cap. A conference hotel may be reimbursed up to 240 credits only when the booking was made before the conference block closed and the conference organizer named that hotel. Personal upgrades are never reimbursable.\n\n5. Ground transport. Rail is preferred. A taxi over 55 credits requires either a transit-disruption record or remote-site travel. A manager's convenience statement is not a disruption record.\n\n6. Pre-approval. A director may pre-approve lodging above the standard cap or taxi use without a disruption. The writing must identify the trip and a maximum amount. Pre-approval cannot waive timeliness, turn a personal upgrade into business expense, alter meal deductions, or override a later amendment.\n\n7. Late claims. Finance must reject an untimely claim unless the portal was unavailable for at least four continuous hours on the submission deadline and the employee submitted by noon on the next business day. Manager absence, missing receipts, workload, or an email sent outside the portal does not extend the period.\n\n8. Case file. Mira traveled to Quarry Point, 112 road kilometers from her home but 34 road kilometers from her assigned office. She departed Monday 6 April 2026, stayed away Monday and Tuesday at midnight, and returned Wednesday 8 April. Her director pre-approved \"up to 230 credits nightly at the Summit Conference Hotel and taxis up to 90 credits for the Quarry Point trip.\" The organizer named Summit and the block was still open when she booked at 225 credits nightly. Tuesday conference registration included breakfast. The accessibility office had documented before travel that the provided breakfast could not meet her medically required diet. A Tuesday taxi cost 82 credits; transit operated normally. Mira entered the complete claim in the portal on Monday 11 May. The portal was available throughout. Company closures in this interval were Friday 10 April and Friday 1 May.\n\n9. Calendar note. The weekend days and the two stated closure Fridays are not business days. All other Mondays through Fridays in the interval are business days.\n\nAMENDMENT A — adopted 15 March 2026, effective for travel beginning on or after 1 April 2026. Despite section 6, director pre-approval cannot raise the lodging cap at a named conference hotel while its conference block remains open. Such lodging is capped at the ordinary 190 credits plus separately stated taxes. The amendment does not change taxi authority or the definition of remote-site travel.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND REFERENCE MATERIAL (part of t