{"expected": "deny_current_export", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-02", "id": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-02", "labels": ["analytics_owner_only", "privacy_only", "privacy_and_security", "deny_current_export"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3357, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The stable cohort token is a direct identifier under Article 2 and the emergency directive. Article 4 flatly bars direct identifiers on the academic route, so adding ordinary privacy and security approvals cannot authorize this proposed university export; it must be denied or redesigned. The paper's aggregate cells do not transform the row-level transfer into an aggregate release.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "privacy_only", "why_hard": "The decisive outcome requires combining a defined term, a baseline rule, a narrow exception, and a later controlling amendment across a long document."}, "question": {"criteria": {"analytics_owner_only": "The case results in analytics owner only under all controlling provisions.", "deny_current_export": "The case results in deny current export under all controlling provisions.", "privacy_and_security": "The case results in privacy and security under all controlling provisions.", "privacy_only": "The case results in privacy only under all controlling provisions."}, "instructions": "Apply the controlling document and select the single authorized outcome for the case file.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "DATA ACCESS STANDARD — 2026 consolidated edition\nArticle 1 — Scope. This standard applies to exports from Atlas, a system containing customer contact, billing, and service telemetry. The decision is made under the version in force when access begins, except a later emergency directive expressly made immediately applicable controls ongoing access.\n\nArticle 2 — Definitions. \"Direct identifier\" includes name, full address, email, telephone number, account number, or any stable value that another company can use to single out a person. A hash is still a direct identifier when it is stable across exports. \"Aggregate\" means every released cell combines at least 25 distinct customers after all filters. \"Internal research\" is work performed only by employees on company-managed devices, with no external recipient or publication. A contractor is external even if issued a company email address. \"Short access\" lasts no more than 30 consecutive days.\n\nArticle 3 — Baseline routes. Aggregate, non-identifying data may be approved by the analytics owner. Row-level telemetry without direct identifiers requires privacy review. Any direct identifier normally requires both privacy and security review, a recorded purpose, and deletion date. Marketing consent is irrelevant to security review.\n\nArticle 4 — Academic collaboration route. A university collaborator may receive row-level telemetry stripped of direct identifiers if a data-use agreement is executed before access, the ethics approval is current, and privacy approves the transformation. The analytics owner cannot substitute for privacy. No direct identifiers may use this route.\n\nArticle 5 — Incident route. During a declared severity-one incident, the incident commander may grant engineers short access to the minimum necessary row-level data. Direct identifiers remain prohibited unless the security duty officer separately approves them. Incident access expires when the incident closes, even if 30 days have not elapsed.\n\nArticle 6 — Renewals. Renewal is a new access decision. Prior review can be relied on only if recipients, fields, purpose, environment, and transformation are unchanged. Adding a field or recipient requires the corresponding current route.\n\nArticle 7 — Case file. Aster University and the company's employee researcher plan a service-reliability study. The university agreement was signed 2 February 2026 and ethics approval runs through December. Privacy approved an export containing event time rounded to the hour, coarse three-digit postal area, device family, failure code, and `cohort_token`, described in the request as SHA-256(account_number + a secret held by the company). The same token would appear for the same account in every monthly export so longitudinal behavior can be joined. Security was copied on the request but gave no approval. The analytics owner approved it. Access is requested for 24 days. There is no declared incident. The employee says the token is anonymous because the university lacks the secret. Minimum cell size in the planned paper is 40, but the university will receive individual rows. No prior export exists.\n\nArticle 8 — Interpretation examples. A randomly regenerated token that changes independently in every export and cannot be linked by the recipient is not stable, though privacy review may still be needed for row-level data. Rounding timestamps does not make individual rows aggregate. Publication cell thresholds govern publication, not the underlying transfer.\n\nEMERGENCY DIRECTIVE 3, issued 20 January 2026. Until withdrawn, stable hashed account values are expressly treated as direct identifiers even if the receiving party cannot reverse the hash or lacks the salt. This clarification applies immediately to new and ongoing access. Academic collaboration may not receive such values. Removal or independently randomized replacement requires a new privacy confirmation.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND REFERENCE MATERIAL (part of the document):\n\nAppendix 1. classification glossary. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated