{"expected": "delete_profile_omar_april_and_table", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-05", "id": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-05", "labels": ["delete_everything", "delete_profile_omar_april_and_table", "delete_only_profile", "delete_nothing"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3232, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "H-22 retains the February and March invoices. H-17 still retains June's March 20 Cedar message, while Omar's April 2 message is both released as to Omar and outside H-17's date range. The memorandum excludes the aggregate table, and the login-only profile is outside H-22, so the profile, Omar April message, and table can be deleted from active storage.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "delete_only_profile", "why_hard": "The decisive outcome requires combining a defined term, a baseline rule, a narrow exception, and a later controlling amendment across a long document."}, "question": {"criteria": {"delete_everything": "The case results in delete everything under all controlling provisions.", "delete_nothing": "The case results in delete nothing under all controlling provisions.", "delete_only_profile": "The case results in delete only profile under all controlling provisions.", "delete_profile_omar_april_and_table": "The case results in delete profile omar april and table under all controlling provisions."}, "instructions": "Apply the controlling document and select the single authorized outcome for the case file.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "RECORDS DELETION AND LEGAL HOLD HANDBOOK\n1. Rule hierarchy. Legal holds override ordinary deletion schedules. A later written release controls the named scope only. The privacy deletion process does not authorize destruction barred by a hold. The clarification memorandum at the end is binding.\n\n2. Definitions. \"Responsive record\" means a record within the custodians, systems, subjects, and time range stated by a hold. Exact duplicates are each responsive when stored in separate systems unless the hold notice permits deduplication. \"Derived analytics\" are records produced from source data; they are not source records. \"Verified deletion\" means deletion from active systems and expiration from ordinary backups under the stated schedule, with a logged confirmation. Cryptographic erasure counts only if the relevant keys exclusively protect the named records.\n\n3. Customer requests. A verified identity request is due within 30 calendar days. Data needed for contract performance may be retained while necessary. Data under legal hold is retained even after the ordinary purpose ends, and the customer receives the standard limitation notice. Non-held data in the same account remains subject to deletion.\n\n4. Backup process. Active copies are deleted by the due date. Immutable backups expire on a 35-day rotation; restoration procedures must reapply deletion markers. The completion record may therefore distinguish active deletion from final backup expiry.\n\n5. Holds. Hold H-17 covers support messages by custodians June and Omar concerning Project Cedar from 1 January through 31 March 2026, including attachments and exports. Hold H-22 covers all billing records for account 448 from 1 February onward. Neither permits deduplication. A legal email may release a hold only if it identifies the hold number and scope.\n\n6. Case file. Account 448 submitted a verified deletion request on 6 April. Contract service ended 5 April. The account contains profile data, invoices dated February and March, support messages with June about Project Cedar dated 20 March, unrelated support messages with Omar dated 2 April, and a dashboard table derived by counting Cedar messages by week. The table contains only week and count. On 10 April Legal wrote: \"Release H-17 for Omar's records only; June remains held. H-22 remains fully active.\" Privacy proposes deleting the profile, every support message, the derived table, and all invoices from active storage on 20 April, leaving deletion markers for backups.\n\n7. Scope application. A release for one custodian does not release another. Records outside a hold's date range are not held merely because a named custodian created them. Derived analytics are covered only if expressly included or if they independently meet the hold's stated record description.\n\n8. Decision options. The deletion team must choose the option that can be executed on 20 April without violating a hold. Ordinary backup expiry may follow later and does not prevent timely active-system action.\n\nCLARIFICATION MEMORANDUM, 12 April. H-22's term \"billing records\" includes invoices and payment ledger entries but not profile contact fields used only for login. H-17's term \"support messages\" does not include aggregate derived tables that contain no message text, attachment, customer identifier, or custodian identifier. This memorandum clarifies existing scope and does not release either hold.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND REFERENCE MATERIAL (part of the document):\n\nAppendix 1. identity verification. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated as the decision maker unless a controlling section expressly says otherwise. Staff should preserve ordinary supporting material and use the defined business calendar. This appendix supplies administration only: it does not enlarge eligibility, erase a deadline, reverse a precedence rule, or create an exception to a requirement stated in the operative sections. Where examples use names or dates, they illustrate recordkeeping and do not amend the operative