{"expected": "east_ward", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-06", "id": "hard-sol-b-long_policy-06", "labels": ["harbor_neighbors", "east_ward", "river_arts", "no_award"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3247, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Harbor lacks a sponsor signature by the deadline, and signatures cannot be created during cure, so it is ineligible. River Arts is eligible under the resolution but its 89 is reduced to 83. East Ward devotes 60,000 of 80,000, exactly 75%, to implementation and scores 84, so it narrowly beats River Arts and clears feasibility.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "river_arts", "why_hard": "The decisive outcome requires combining a defined term, a baseline rule, a narrow exception, and a later controlling amendment across a long document."}, "question": {"criteria": {"east_ward": "The case results in east ward under all controlling provisions.", "harbor_neighbors": "The case results in harbor neighbors under all controlling provisions.", "no_award": "The case results in no award under all controlling provisions.", "river_arts": "The case results in river arts under all controlling provisions."}, "instructions": "Apply the controlling document and select the single authorized outcome for the case file.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "COMMUNITY GRANT AWARD RULEBOOK\nPart 1 — Purpose and precedence. The program awards small implementation grants. Eligibility gates are applied before scoring; an ineligible application cannot win regardless of score. The board resolution at the end postdates this edition and overrides conflicting provisions.\n\nPart 2 — Entities. An eligible lead must be a registered nonprofit or a municipal department. An unincorporated group may participate but cannot lead or receive funds. A fiscal sponsor counts as lead only if it signs the application before the deadline and accepts financial responsibility. Two entities under common control count as one applicant for the one-award limit.\n\nPart 3 — Timing. Applications are due 17:00 local program time on 30 June. Portal receipts control. A submission may be cured within two business days only for a missing attachment that existed before the deadline; signatures, budgets, and partner commitments cannot be created during cure. A portal outage extension requires an official outage of at least 30 continuous minutes during the final two hours.\n\nPart 4 — Budget. Requests must be 20,000 through 80,000 credits inclusive. At least 70 percent must be implementation cost. Administrative cost is any general management, accounting, rent, or fundraising amount. Equipment used directly to deliver the project is implementation. Contingency is administrative unless allocated to named implementation tasks.\n\nPart 5 — Conflicts. A reviewer with a financial relationship to any applicant or named delivery partner must recuse. Mere residence in the served municipality is not a conflict. A recused reviewer's score is discarded, not averaged as zero.\n\nPart 6 — Scoring. Eligible applications receive impact up to 40, feasibility up to 30, equity up to 20, and evidence up to 10. Normally the highest total wins. A proposal below 18 on feasibility cannot win. Ties are broken by higher equity, then lower requested amount.\n\nPart 7 — Case file. Three proposals remain. Harbor Neighbors is unincorporated and lists Bright Civic Foundation as fiscal sponsor, but Bright signed at 09:00 on 1 July; Harbor's portal receipt was 29 June. It requests 40,000, with 31,000 implementation. Its valid non-conflicted scores total 91 and feasibility 27. East Ward is a municipal department, submitted on time, requests 80,000, with 55,000 named implementation equipment and work, 5,000 named implementation training, and 20,000 general administration. Its scores are impact 34, feasibility 24, equity 18, evidence 8, total 84. River Arts is a registered nonprofit under common control with last year's winning nonprofit River Center. It submitted on time, requests 62,000 with 50,000 implementation, and scores total 89 with feasibility 26. No portal outage occurred.\n\nPart 8 — Prior awards. The ordinary one-award limit says the same applicant cannot receive awards in consecutive program years. The published FAQ once stated that separately registered affiliates are always separate; FAQs are explanatory and not amendments.\n\nBOARD RESOLUTION 26-4, adopted 15 May. For the 2026 round only, entities under common control with a prior-year winner are eligible to apply, but their total score is reduced by six points after all reviewer-conflict adjustments. This replaces exclusion under the consecutive-year rule; it does not change the definition of common control. Also, for 2026 only, the implementation minimum is 75 percent, not 70 percent. All other gates remain.\n\nADMINISTRATIVE AND REFERENCE MATERIAL (part of the document):\n\nAppendix 1. portal receipts. The organization keeps a contemporaneous record for this topic. Routine records identify the owner, the date recorded, the system of record, and any later correction. A draft note is not an approval, silence is not consent, and a copied recipient is not treated as the decision maker unless a controlling section expressly says otherwise. Staff should preserve ordinary supporting material and use the defined business calendar. This appendix supplies administration only: it does not enlarge eligibility, erase a deadline, reverse a precedence rule, or create an exception to a requirement stated in the operative sections. Where examples use names or dates, they illustrate recordkeeping and do not amend the operative case. Historical versions remain searchable for audit purposes, but a superseded version has no prospective effect. Questions a