{"expected": "no", "family": "judge_hard", "group": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-03", "id": "hard-sol-c-judge_hard-03", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 320, "author_model": "gpt-5.6-sol", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Although COUNT(i.id) is appropriate, predicates on i in WHERE reject the NULL-extended rows, turning the LEFT JOIN into an effective inner join. Customers with no qualifying invoice disappear; the predicates belong in ON.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "A polished response differs from a fully correct one by a small technical or constraint-level detail."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The response contains any substantive error or misses an explicit constraint.", "true": "The response is fully correct and satisfies every explicit constraint."}, "instructions": "Does the response fully and correctly satisfy every explicit requirement in the request? Judge the derivation, code, result, and formatting, not merely whether the final number looks plausible.", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": {"request": "In PostgreSQL, list every customer exactly once with the count of that customer's paid invoices dated in 2025, including customers with zero. Tables are customers(id,name) and invoices(id,customer_id,status,issued_at). Provide one SQL query; do not use a correlated subquery.\n\nAdditional evaluation context: The report feeds a customer-retention dashboard, so absence of invoices is meaningful and must appear as a numerical zero. Assume customer IDs are unique, invoice IDs are non-null, timestamps are stored consistently, and multiple qualifying invoices may exist. The query should remain valid when the invoices table has no matching row at all. The answer will be reviewed as a complete handoff, so correctness of intermediate reasoning, units, boundary conventions, and every stated presentation constraint matters independently of whether the concluding value appears plausible.", "response": "```sql\nSELECT c.id, c.name, COUNT(i.id) AS paid_invoice_count\nFROM customers AS c\nLEFT JOIN invoices AS i ON i.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE i.status = 'paid'\n  AND i.issued_at >= DATE '2025-01-01'\n  AND i.issued_at <  DATE '2026-01-01'\nGROUP BY c.id, c.name\nORDER BY c.id;\n```\n`COUNT(i.id)` avoids counting the NULL-extended row produced by the outer join."}}
